decisions.detail:
Option A explicitly selects first-party implementation using existing runtime IO. It is a proposal, not presumed authorization.

## Exact independent API choice

```text
open(endpoint: String, queue: String) core.jobs.JobQueue !core.service.ServiceError
```

This call returns the existing JobQueue family, not a Redis-specific queue lifecycle.

Both arguments are required and nonempty.

Invalid or unavailable store opening uses the existing queue error carrier; it does not fabricate a working handle.

The exact existing enqueue, claim, acknowledge, recover_expired, receipt, events and status calls in the complete fixture are unchanged.

D-DX-QUEUE1=A keeps SQLite as default, explicit provider selection, Prelude-owned leases/retries and at-least-once effects.

The store must satisfy the existing typed durable-store transaction contract or be rejected.

No implicit SQLite fallback, hidden duplicate enqueue or second receipt identity is allowed.

Authored Redis recovery remains unproved; approval of this API does not certify a nonconforming implementation.

Exact current native enqueue returns Result<JetJobQueueReceipt, JetServiceError> at JobQueueNative.rs:49-61; Core.jet:64 exports JobQueue and does not export a new queue-specific error.

ServiceError is the existing service carrier, not JobError payload data.

Core.jet:85 exports core.service.ServiceError.

This exact existing error type is preserved.

Dependency disposition: neither option adds a Jet-linked third-party library, SDK, background broker, package installer, production endpoint or automatic download.

A is a first-party binding using existing runtime IO and the explicit protocol contract.

External broker servers are caller-selected services, never a stock default.

B leaves the complete peer adapter outside Jet.

Peer npm packages and versions are comparator fixture inputs only.

This is an explicit no-adoption boundary, not permission to choose an unnamed dependency later.

Any implementation that needs a new third-party dependency is outside this approved candidate contract.

The location choice remains D-NEST-REMOTEQUEUE1. Its import alias in this example follows optional-package option A only for readability. The public member names, arguments, return carriers, error cases and lifecycle above are the API choice, regardless of the separately selected location. No placement choice silently approves this API.

A approves this exact contract. B definitively adds no Jet protocol API and uses the complete external peer program below. B is not a promise of a future binding. The earlier fixture text that asks for a separate API gate refers to this ballot; it does not create another postponed API decision. The exact A call below supersedes earlier illustrative call-return annotations where they differ.

