The earlier local Jet examples set one trace value directly.

They prove no propagation and remain current-source examples, NOT RUN.

The following authored two-process producer replaces the fixed-value producer for the shared export workload.

Only the client chooses the trace identifier.

Orders receives it from the request header and forwards it over HTTP to invoices.

Each service extracts its own received header and logs that identifier.

Neither service assigns the client trace constant.

This exercises trace-header transport, not complete distributed span creation or automatic Jet context propagation.

Use fresh isolated files.

Start `node services.mjs invoices-api`, then `node services.mjs orders-api`; wait for both listeners.

Run `node client.mjs`.

Stop the two service processes after the response, without deleting their log files.

Start the corrected receiver.mjs, then run the retained sender against both files, or the exact proposed Jet export program.

Expected selected records contain the one client trace ID and both service names, with no token field.

Start a second isolated fixture with a different nonzero client trace identifier; both output records must change with it.

A hard-coded producer would fail this boundary.

Current Jet request-to-log context propagation remains unproved and is scoped to #3088.

All programs and expected outputs are authored NOT RUN. No network, request context, export, failure or privacy result was executed. The exact custom JSON POST protocol at `/events` is not OTLP, Observe ingestion or a hosted service. Its receiver is part of this proposal, not an interoperability claim.

```javascript
// services.mjs. AUTHORED NOT RUN. Run once per role.
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
import { appendFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
const service = process.argv[2];
if (!['orders-api', 'invoices-api'].includes(service)) throw new Error('invalid role');
const port = service === 'orders-api' ? 3001 : 3002;
const server = createServer(async (req, res) => {
  if (req.method !== 'POST' || req.url !== '/order') { res.writeHead(404).end(); return; }
  const header = req.headers.traceparent;
  const match = typeof header === 'string' && /^00-([0-9a-f]{32})-([0-9a-f]{16})-01$/.exec(header);
  if (!match || /^0+$/.test(match[1]) || /^0+$/.test(match[2])) { res.writeHead(400).end(); return; }
  try {
    if (service === 'orders-api') {
      const downstream = await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3002/order', {
        method: 'POST', headers: { traceparent: header }, signal: AbortSignal.timeout(1000),
      });
      if (downstream.status !== 200 || await downstream.text() !== 'order-41') {
        res.writeHead(502).end(); return;
      }
    }
    const record = { trace_id: match[1], service, order: 'order-41', token: '[redacted]' };
    await appendFile(`${service}.jsonl`, JSON.stringify(record) + '\n');
    res.writeHead(200).end('order-41');
  } catch { res.writeHead(502).end(); }
});
server.listen(port, '127.0.0.1');
```

